{66} By "the human being" here is meant everyone who denies the Resurrection and considers its occurrence far-fetched. Asking in the form of negation, obstinacy and disbelief, he says: {"When I am dead, shall I indeed be brought forth alive?"}; that is: how could God bring me back to life after death, and after I have become decayed bones? This cannot happen and cannot be conceived! This is according to his corrupt reason, his evil intention, and his obstinacy toward the messengers of God and His Books. Had he looked with even the slightest look and reflected with even the slightest reflection, he would have seen that his denial of the Resurrection is in the utmost absurdity.

{67} For this reason, He, may He be exalted, mentioned a decisive proof and a clear evidence, known to everyone, for the possibility of the Resurrection, saying: {"Does not man remember that We created him before, when he was nothing?"}; that is: does his mind not turn to and recall his first state, and that God created him the first time when he was nothing? Then, the One who was able to create him from nothingness, when he was not a thing worth mentioning, is surely able to bring him into being again after he has been torn apart, and to gather him together after he has been scattered? This is like His saying: {"It is He who originates creation and then repeats it, and that is easier for Him."}

In His saying {"Does not man remember"} there is a call to reflect upon the rational proof in the gentlest of address, and that the denial of whoever denies this is based on his heedlessness of his original state; for had he remembered it and brought it to mind, he would not have denied it.

